sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS and R1-R4, agent-observer-swamp-acts, single-swamp-node. docs/reference/private-dns.md#resolver-selection.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 20, 22 (the live policy's match guards the set; the end check resolves names).
  %% Ported 2026-11-03: the tunnel runs as wg-quick, so the PostUp hook and marker persist the policy, not a networkd drop-in.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · routing domains change
  %% kind: fleet.peer_dns
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  Note over GW: Domains changed<br/>task #5163 · peer Node 12
  %%> When:
  %%> - Node 12's policy differs from the one its last successful run applied, for example a Node with a new TLD joins or the `vpn` role moves
  %%> - the Gateway plan names one changed peer at a time; Swamp's minute maintenance admits it once per policy
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - no hourly re-run; host drift with an unchanged policy waits for doctor and a reconcile
  %%> - a full re-apply of Node 12's footprint is `node.converge`, which runs this workflow as a child
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - Node 12 is an active managed Linux peer with roles; the `vpn` Node and the Gateway stay out
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5163 recorded with the domains, the DNS server and the private name to resolve
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-fleet-peer-dns
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the routing domains, such as `~orbit`, the DNS server's WireGuard address and the wg-quick hook line
  %%> - the private name to resolve, `gateway.orbit`
  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  SW->>N: Keep the DNS hook<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/line
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - keeps the Gateway-rendered `PostUp = resolvectl …` line in `orbit.conf`, so the policy returns when the tunnel starts
  %%> - leaves every other line as it is; changes nothing when the line matches
  SW->>N: Write the DNS marker<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - records the link, server and domains in `/etc/wireguard/orbit.dns-link`; changes nothing when it matches
  SW->>N: Set the DNS policy<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/exec · notIf match
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one command sets the server and routing domains on `orbit` with `default-route no`; only Orbit names go to VPN DNS
  %%> When it matches:
  %%> - the `notIf` guard reads the live `orbit` link; when it already holds the policy, the command does not run
  SW->>N: Check resolution<br/>resolvectl query
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - resolves `gateway.orbit`, which must answer through `orbit`, and `example.com`, which must not
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the policy only counts as applied when Node 12 resolves both
  alt both names resolve
    N-->>SW: private + public ok
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the DNS policy<br/>Node 12 · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5163 succeeds and records the applied server and domains
    %%> - the plan names the next changed peer
  else setting or checking fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no answer
    %%> When:
    %%> - a write or `resolvectl` refuses, SSH fails, or a name does not resolve where it should
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark the peer failed<br/>stop · rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5163 fails; the plan keeps naming Node 12, so later peers wait
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - safe: `orbit:peer-dns-reconcile` admits a new task, and matching steps change nothing
  end
